  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>The ownership path most embedded jobs hide.</strong> After a few years of delivered products, an engineer who can take a startup's idea to a working board
      can sell that as a fixed-price package: schematic review, firmware, test jig. It scales because you set the price, keep the reusable parts, and can later hire or train others.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="entry">Degree, diploma or skills: the entry routes</h2>

  <p>
    A degree is not a legal requirement for embedded work, but many employers filter on it, and some public-sector posts require one. The problem is degree-only thinking.
    For college spending, keep roughly 10% of the family education budget for the fee, and treat anything above about 20% as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence. Boards, a basic logic analyser and test equipment are small costs next to a fee.
    Avoid education debt for a short course.
  </p>

