  <p class="note-line">
    Pay figures here are indicative ranges and averages from public profiles, not exact or guaranteed amounts. Pay differs by city, employer, skill and year, and markets change. Check current job listings (for example on LinkedIn, Naukri or Indeed) and ask people working in the role before you decide.
  </p>

  <p>
    One guide claims that functional-safety skills such as ISO 26262 and automotive domain depth lift pay by 20 to 40%.
    That is the guide's own claim and we could not verify it. The safer reading: the engineers who earn at the top of any band tend to have a domain and proof.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI, code assistants and EDA</h2>

  <p>
    Code assistants can draft a driver, explain a datasheet table and help read logs. Practitioners report that they are weaker on timing, register details and interrupt behaviour,
    and that compiled code still fails on the real board. These are practitioner accounts, not a survey.
    Safety-critical logic needs human authors and formal checks.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Under pressure</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Boilerplate drivers and simple scripts, so the entry-level coding task shrinks first.</li>
        <li>Routine testbench and report generation.</li>
        <li>Generic embedded tutorials as proof of skill.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Still rewarded</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Finding why a board resets under load.</li>
        <li>Choosing what to verify in a chip block, and judging coverage.</li>
        <li>Reading a customer failure and tracing it back to a cause.</li>
        <li>Signing off on safety-relevant code.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A staged path: now, use an assistant to explain datasheets and draft test code, then check every line on the board or simulator.
    Next, build a small automated check that runs on every code change. Later, as chip-design tools add AI features and edge AI spreads,
    learn to run small models on microcontrollers; the skills council already lists an AI Embedded Product Developer qualification pack (ELE/Q1407).
    The market is early, so say plainly that AI leverage is practical for drafting and checking, and still thin for hands-on bring-up.
    For the sector-wide view, read <a href="/blog/ai-future/ai-impact-on-it-jobs-india/">AI impact on IT jobs in India</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="business">Who pays, and how they trust you</h2>

  <p>
    In a salaried role your employer pays and hires through interviews, so the sales layer matters mainly for the side route and your next move.
    For freelance or small projects, the payer is a hardware startup, a maker brand or a small manufacturer that has a product idea and no firmware person.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Positioning:</strong> say who you help and with what. "I bring up sensor boards and write the first firmware for small IoT products" beats "I do embedded".</li>
    <li><strong>Proof:</strong> a repository, a short demo video, a photo of the board and a captured signal, plus one referral they can ask.</li>
    <li><strong>First skill to learn:</strong> a simple fixed-scope offer and a calm price conversation. Ask what a delayed product costs them before you quote.</li>
    <li><strong>One number to watch:</strong> how many conversations turn into a paid pilot.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Be honest about the limits. Hardware work needs a board in hand, some clients ask for NDAs, and many businesses prefer a firm over an individual. Start with one small paid task, then ask for a referral.
    Clear English writing for bug reports and a small network help here too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="proof">Proof that a hardware recruiter can check</h2>

  <p>
    Show work early on one platform. For embedded that usually means a GitHub repository per project, with a README, wiring photo, schematic or open-hardware files and a short demo clip.
    One finished repository beats five abandoned ones, and a polite message to a working engineer with that link starts better conversations than a bare request for help.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">Pass The 3 Gates with one board project before you call yourself job-ready.</p>
